  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1218 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nIt was a seasonably chilly but otherwise quiet Thanksgiving over\neastern NE/western IA as northerly flow aloft continued its \nhold on our area. Our quiet weather comes in sharp contrast to \nthat of locations not too far east, where a strong closed low, \npresently situated near Cincinnati, produced snowfall \naccumulations in excess of 10\" in parts of Missouri and \nIllinois, as well as heavy rain and gusty winds for much of the \nGreat Lakes. As that cyclone continues to lift further \nnorth/east of our area and a weak ridge builds overnight, these \ncalm conditions should continue. With model soundings suggesting\nsaturation near the surface Friday morning, some freezing fog \nmay develop, although it would likely be patchy and confined to \nriver valleys due to the shallow nature of the saturated layer \nand stronger winds just above the surface. \n\nSurface flow will become southerly throughout the day Friday,\nleading to warmer conditions areawide, with high temperatures \nin the upper 40s to low 50s. During the afternoon, an upper-\nlevel shortwave trough and associated surface pressure trough\nwill traverse the CWA. Recent guidance is suggesting that the\nnear-sfc layer may not be as dry as previously thought, and thus\nchances for rain, while still fairly low, have increased \nslightly with this forecast cycle. The highest chances for rain \n(around 20%) will be north of I-80, where near-sfc dry air will \nbe a bit easier for falling rain to saturate.\n\nBetter chances for rain will come with a stronger system Saturday\nafternoon and evening. Another trough will develop early \nSaturday morning over the northern Rockies, swinging southeast\nand strengthening throughout the day. As this trough approaches\nthe mid-Missouri River Valley, a band of enhanced 700-850-mb \nfrontogenesis will develop, leading to the development of a band\nof rain over central and eastern Nebraska by early Saturday\nafternoon, expanding into Iowa by early evening (75-95% PoP).\nThe rain in this band is not expected to be particularly heavy,\nwith the highest storm totals likely to be under half an inch.\nRain will end from west to east Saturday night, exiting our far\neastern counties by early Sunday morning. There is also a very\nslight chance of a snowflake or two mixing in on the backside of\nthe rainband, but this will be far from enough to cause any kind\nof impact. As the surface cold front passes through our area\njust behind the rainband, and wet roadways may freeze and remain\nslippery into Sunday morning.\n\nA surface cold front will pass as the rain ends, bringing with\nit a much cooler Sunday, with highs in the upper 30s to lower\n40s across the CWA. However, as flow becomes more southwesterly\nduring the early part of next week, a warming trend should\ncommence, with afternoon highs by Wednesday in the 50s areawide.\nBy later next week, extended range guidance is suggesting a \ndeep upper-level trough may move into the central CONUS, which \nmay bring our next chance of widespread precipitation.",
